 ## Overview
-This family encompasses methods for visualizing the boundary defined by a specific scalar value (isovalue) within a volumetric dataset. It solves the problem of conveying shape and depth from implicit data by either extracting an explicit polygonal mesh or computing surface intersections on-the-fly via raymarching [S1, S3, unofficial]. It is distinguished from direct volume rendering by isolating the specific isosurface rather than accumulating light through the entire volume, and from general surface extraction by focusing on the final visual representation pipeline [S3, unofficial].
+This family encompasses methods for visualizing isosurfaces extracted from volumetric scalar fields, ranging from direct procedural ray-marching to explicit rasterization of pre-computed meshes. Unlike full volumetric rendering which accumulates light through the entire volume, these techniques isolate specific density thresholds to reveal internal structures or material boundaries [S4, unofficial][S2]. The primary distinction among siblings lies in the representation: [Isosurface Rendering](7669-isosurface-rendering.md) uses procedural ray marching for smooth, continuous appearance without explicit geometry, while [Triangular Mesh Scene Rendering](4644-triangular-mesh-scene-rendering.md) and [Optimized Mesh Reference](1888-optimized-mesh-reference.md) rely on explicit triangle meshes for standard rasterization [S1, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. [Multi-Isosurface Rendering](8745-multi-isosurface-rendering.md) extends this by extracting and managing a subset of representative surfaces at different isovalue levels for interactive exploration [S2].
 
